  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nAs the surface high continues to move east this afternoon, a \nweak disturbance aloft will lift the stalled front in the west \nand produce some weak isentropic lift. This has brought \nincreased clouds and a few very light sprinkles to areas out\nwest. An increased gradient at the surface will bring some \nbreezy southerly winds. Slightly warmer temperatures will be \nfound in the west and the warmer side of the elevated front, \nwith highs near 70 degrees. Otherwise areas east will generally \nsee highs in the 60s. \n\nA surface low will move through tonight into tomorrow. As a \nresult more scattered light rain showers or sprinkles are \npossible tonight through much of the day tomorrow. Behind the \nweak cold front wind will shift to be breezy and from the northwest\nin the early afternoon tomorrow. Dry conditions are expected\nbehind the cold front. Low RH combined with breezy northwest \nwinds could bring some increased fire weather concern Saturday \nafternoon and early evening for areas in the far southwest. \nHigh temperatures will be in the 60s to lower 70s Saturday. \n\nSaturday night through Sunday high pressure will return\nweakening winds, with clear skies, and cooler overnight lows in\nthe 30s to lower 40s. Sunday, look for seasonable highs in the\n60s to near 70 along with light winds. As we move into next\nweek, there will be a brief warming period due to a building \nridge centered in the Southern Plains raising high temperatures\ninto the upper 70s to low 80s by Monday. Breezy south winds \nreturn to areas out east Monday afternoon. Winds combined with\nlow RH bring back increased fire weather concerns Tuesday,\nagain for areas focused to the southwest.",
